info.description:
The Getrak API provides access to Getrak's tracking data, as well as other infromation generated on top of it.

This documentation provides information about each endpoint in the API. The right-hand pane of this page show example
requests and responses for each endpoint in one or more languages (like cURL, Node.js/JavaScript or PHP).

# Getting started

Follow there steps to start interacting:

1. Get an API client ID and secret.
2. Authenticate to the API to get a token. Authentication is requires for all endpoints in Getrak API.The API accepts
[OAuth2](https://oauth.net/2/) for all endpoints.

The token is valid for a period of time during which it can be used repeatedly without the need to issue a new one.
Excessive token issuance may be limited and blocked in certain cases.

After you have authenticated to the API, you can send requests to the API using any programming language or program
that can send HTTP requests. The base URL for all endpoints is `https://api.getrak.com`.

```javascript
const axios = require('axios')

axios.post('https://api.getrak.com/newkoauth/oauth/token', {
  {
    grant_type: 'password',
    username: 'joe@my-company',
    password: 'my-super-secure-password',

    // Or refresh token
    // grant_type: 'refresh_token',
    // refresh_token: 'my-valid-refresh-token',
  },
}, {
  // Using credentials separatelly
  auth: { username: 'ClientID', password: 'ClientSecret' },
  headers: {
    // Or using the credentials base64 as a header
    // Authorization: 'Basic Q2xpZW50SUQ6Q2xpZW50U2VjcmV0',

    'Content-Type': 'multipart/form-data; charset=UTF-8',
  },
})
  .then((response) => {
    // The object will contain the `access_token` and `expires_in`
    // properties, representing the token and how long it can be used
    // before a new one need to be retrieved. If refresh token is enable,
    // the `refresh_token` property will also be present.
    const {
      access_token: accessToken,
      refresh_token: refreshToken,
      expires_in: expiresIn,
    } = response.data

    console.log(accessToken, refreshToken, expiresIn)
  })
  .catch((err) => {
    console.error(err)
  })
```

# Paging results

Many endpoints, including search endpoints, split long response data into multiplpe pages with the `page` and
`per_page` parameters. For example, if a search result has 20 items, you can retrieve items 11-15 by using `page=3`
and `per_page=3` in the request, as shown in the examples in the right-hand pane. For legacy APIs you can show up to
500 results per page and for modern ones 100 results per page. Some endpoints have different limits; see the
description of the `per_page` parameter for the individual endpoints.

# Responses

Successful requests return a 200, 201, or 204 response code.

Most oendpoints return information in JSON format. Most programming languages have libraries that can accept and
manipulate JSON data.

Sobe API client may return a limited set of results.

## Errors

The API returns error codes such as 400, 403, 404, and 500 along with information about the error. Here are some
common error responses:

A `400 Bad Request` response often means that the request body is improperly formatted or missing required fields.

A `401 Unauthorized` response often means that the request did not include a valid OAuth2 token or basic
authentication credentials.

A `403 Forbidden` response can mean that the token in the request does not contain the scopes that the endpoint
requires.

A `404 Not Found` response can mean that the media, license, or collection you are trying to access does not exist.

A `500 Internal Server Error `response means that an error happened on the server. This can mean that the request is
not valid, such as if you try to delete or edit a collection that does not exist, or if the API otherwise can't do
what you requested. It can also mean a temporary problem with the API. Check your request and try again or contact us.

## Rate limits

All applications are limited to a certain amount of requests per minute. If your application exceeds its limit, the API
returns an error response with a status code of `429 Too Many Requests`.

